WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Accounting and Reporting of Defect Opportunities

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.800
Welcome back to this session on defect opportunities. What we would like to do,

3
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.433
is to advance this concept one stage further,

4
00:00:14.433 --> 00:00:20.366
by considering the metric defects per opportunity,

5
00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:25.999
as a simple ratio. Now, to understand that I'm going to create some slots.

6
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:31.833
Let's say, three simple slots. And those slots belong to the numerator.

7
00:00:31.833 --> 00:00:38.866
So, I’m going to form a ratio here of some slots

8
00:00:38.866 --> 00:00:44.366
in the numerator

9
00:00:44.366 --> 00:00:47.932
and some slots in the denominator.

10
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:52.399
So, we got denominator slots, numerator slots.

11
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:58.733
And, every slot in the denominator is an event.

12
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:04.833
It's the occurrence of an interface between

13
00:01:04.833 --> 00:01:10.933
the process capability and the design requirement.

14
00:01:10.933 --> 00:01:15.133
The upper and lower specification. In other words, it's the marriage

15
00:01:15.133 --> 00:01:19.566
between the process and the design.

16
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:22.932
If the marriage occurred,

17
00:01:22.933 --> 00:01:27.933
then we would have a one in this box.

18
00:01:27.933 --> 00:01:30.933
In other words, there is a production opportunity.

19
00:01:30.933 --> 00:01:37.233
If the process cycles once, then it creates,

20
00:01:37.233 --> 00:01:41.866
it creates an outcome. Now, that outcome

21
00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:45.932
could be one of two possible conditions.

22
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:51.799
It could either be a zero, or it could be a one.

23
00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:57.500
In other words, if it's a one, then it's a defect.

24
00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:02.600
That interface was not good. It didn't pass.

25
00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:07.300
It wasn't up to standard. It wasn't up to par. A defect was created.

26
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.733
If it's a zero, then it was yield.

27
00:02:10.733 --> 00:02:15.733
So, if it's zero. It's a green. It's in the spec boundary.

28
00:02:15.733 --> 00:02:22.266
If it's a one, it's out here in these tails. So, the first thing is, did we create it? Yes.

29
00:02:22.266 --> 00:02:29.232
See, that's the first question, did we create it?

30
00:02:29.233 --> 00:02:34.866
Yes equals one, no equals zero.

31
00:02:34.866 --> 00:02:40.632
In this case, it’s a one so we did create it. Now we say,

32
00:02:40.633 --> 00:02:46.899
if yes, was it good? That has two possible conditions 0, 1.

33
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:50.466
So, we can get a 0,1 here, 0,1 here.

34
00:02:50.466 --> 00:02:57.299
Now, let's suppose on the first cycle of the process we did in fact create a CTQ,

35
00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:03.500
a critical to quality characteristic. We did create it so there's a 1. And now let's suppose,

36
00:03:03.500 --> 00:03:10.533
strictly for the sake of argument, that it was not defective. So, we put in a zero.

37
00:03:10.533 --> 00:03:14.733
Now let's suppose that we go back on the second cycle of the process,

38
00:03:14.733 --> 00:03:17.633
the process cycles again.

39
00:03:17.633 --> 00:03:24.333
And let's suppose, that we created another CTQ, a replication of the first CTQ.

40
00:03:24.333 --> 00:03:31.299
We’ve got production going on. So, we made the first CTQ. We’ve made that CTQ again.

41
00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:38.266
But this time, aw-oh, we had a failure. So, we get a 1.

42
00:03:38.266 --> 00:03:43.466
And now let's suppose, on the third cycle, we left the material out of the machine.

43
00:03:43.466 --> 00:03:50.432
And so, it didn't create the CTQ. Therefore, since it’s a zero here, it has to be a zero there.

44
00:03:50.433 --> 00:03:55.733
You can't create a defect if you never made the CTQ. That simple.

45
00:03:55.733 --> 00:04:02.599
Now, let's take a look across the numerator here. We’re going to come over here.

46
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:09.466
How many defects were made? 0, 1, 0. 1.

47
00:04:09.466 --> 00:04:14.832
How many opportunities were created? 2.

48
00:04:14.833 --> 00:04:17.999
So, what's our defects per opportunity?

49
00:04:18.000 --> 00:04:25.000
Defects per opportunity equals 1 over 2, which is equal to .50.

50
00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:28.333
In this case, 50%

51
00:04:28.333 --> 00:04:33.766
of the opportunities we’re creating were defective.

52
00:04:33.766 --> 00:04:38.666
Now, what we do not want to do is this.

53
00:04:38.666 --> 00:04:43.932
We do not want to say, well, the process cycled three times. One, two, three.

54
00:04:43.933 --> 00:04:47.833
See, here’s the process cycles. Cycle one. Cycle two, Cycle three.

55
00:04:47.833 --> 00:04:50.799
One, plus one, plus one, equals three.

56
00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.600
So, the defects per opportunity is one over three. Not one over two. Wrong.

57
00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:02.666
We’re not measuring defects per process cycle.

58
00:05:02.666 --> 00:05:09.532
We are measuring defects per product opportunity, per CTQ.

59
00:05:09.533 --> 00:05:12.566
So once again,

60
00:05:12.566 --> 00:05:19.566
a defect opportunity is anything that we regularly measure and report.

61
00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:24.566
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
